    "storyBody": "In the last comprehensive practical before the spring floods, Ji Heng had to deliver a cylinder of weather records to the outer-reef lighthouse before low tide, then return through the northern channel. Pass this test and he could still take a spring trainee berth on a merchant vessel; miss it, and he would wait another six months. Ji Heng’s chart work and tidal calculations had always been the best in the class. On admission, he brought a deck-completion record from his former sailing school and, in front of the instructor, tied the required knot correctly three times in a row with a practice rope. On that evidence, the school exempted him from the month-long deck fundamentals course. From then on, the exemption card stayed tucked inside the chart folder he used most.\n\nFor the next few weeks, the exemption seemed to carry no cost at all. Navigation training was divided by role: the duty sailors prepared the mooring lines, sheets, and hoisting lines, while the students judged wind, tide, and route. Twice Ji Heng altered course for a cross-current and reached the marker ahead of the others in his group. Once, during a sail change, a loose rope end was casually retied by a sailor while Ji Heng looked down to check his position; another time, before a lamp was hoisted, a sailor tightened the loaded knot first. The ropes Ji Heng took over were always already ready to work. The exemption card in his chart folder looked more and more like proof that the decision had been right.\n\nThe comprehensive practical had different rules: from departure to return, the student had to handle everything alone, and shore staff were not allowed to touch the ropes for him. The forecast clearly said the crosswind would strengthen after ten, and Ji Heng had already marked “reef before the red buoy” on his route. The first half went exactly to plan. At the red buoy, wearing wet gloves, he wrapped the damp sheet around the cleat and tied the same knot he had used in his entrance test. As soon as the sail came under load again, the rope end slowly slipped back half a finger-width. He untied it and tried again. At rest it looked neat; under load it began to slide once more. The line on the chart was correct, but the sail he had failed to shorten was pushing the bow toward the pale water over the shallows.\n\nJi Heng first pinned the rope end with the heel of his hand, still trying to get the record cylinder to the lighthouse. But the moment he released it to reach for the hoisting line, the rope slid farther and the boat swung broadside; another examination boat was approaching behind him along the same safe line. He shoved the cylinder back into the cabin, turned the helm into the wind, and raised the return flag. By the time he reached harbor, his tide window had passed. The test was recorded as incomplete, and the spring trainee berth went to the reserve student. The instructor did not change the equipment. Using Ji Heng’s same wet rope and the same cleat, he tied the knot and had three people pull backward together; it did not move. Then he asked Ji Heng to tie it once more on an unloaded rope. Ji Heng still performed every step correctly.\n\nJi Heng did not redraw his charts or do more paper exercises. He took the exemption card out and carried it, together with his charts, into the ground-floor deck room. For the next four weeks, he practiced first with dry rope, then with wet rope, thick gloves, and different directions of load. Sometimes the instructor put the sail under tension first and only then told him which knot to change to; sometimes he made Ji Heng switch continuously among reefing, mooring, and hoisting a lamp. When Ji Heng moved too slowly, the rope immediately bit into his glove. When he chose poorly, the model boat drifted off its marked line. More than once, the bell for the chart class upstairs had already rung while he was still untying the knot he had just made and starting again under a different condition.\n\nThe second practical again followed the outer reef, and the forecast crosswind was stronger than before. Before the red buoy, Ji Heng reefed the sail; once the wet rope took the load, it stayed where he had set it. Near the lighthouse, he changed hands to reach for the hoisting line, and the rope end on the cleat did not move. When he signed back in at harbor, a new passing stamp was pressed onto the inside cover of his chart folder. Ji Heng folded the old exemption card into a narrow strip and wrapped it around the rolled chart. Then, with the rope he had just taken off the deck, he tied the two together.",
