    "moduleZh": {
      "intro": "三个人可以把同一批材料概括得几乎一样，却对“这能证明什么”给出不同答案。这个故事把一般阅读和学科读写区分开：真正专业的阅读，不只问材料说了什么，还要问这个领域凭什么相信它。",
      "diagramTitle": "从读懂材料到按学科规则判断主张",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "先准确理解材料表面的事实与主张",
        "用本学科的问题重新分配注意力",
        "按领域标准检查证据的来源与条件",
        "限定证据能够支持的推断范围",
        "用与证据强度相称的方式重新表达结论"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "一般理解是起点：先读对日期、数字、事件和字面意思。",
        "不同学科随后会关注来源与时序、测量条件、符号关系、文件权限等不同特征。",
        "学科读写不是多找细节，而是知道本领域为什么把某些细节当作可信度和相关性的关键。",
        "即使所有事实都真实，证据也可能不足以支持更强的因果、责任或解释性结论。",
        "最终写作要随证据改变断言强度，并明确未知部分和还需要什么材料。"
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "三名实习教师写出的三十字摘要几乎一样，日期和数值也都正确 → 一般阅读能力可以准确理解材料，但还没有进入各学科的证据判断。",
        "安岚检查报道日期、来源和说法出现的时间 → 历史性阅读关注来源、时序和情境，以判断一项说法如何产生。",
        "陶宇确认数值无误后仍检查采样点、天气和推断范围 → 科学阅读不仅看数字对不对，还看测量条件决定数据能说明什么。",
        "米珂确认罚款真实，却区分申报违规与污染责任认定 → 法律与公民领域要判断文件权限、规则性质和证明效力。",
        "增加会议纪要和第二次检测后仍不能写出“元凶” → 学科化判断不是普通事实核查或简单增加材料，而是按领域标准限制主张。"
      ],
      "boundary": "学科读写能力并不否定通用阅读策略，也不是说每个学科只有一种固定读法。不同任务、子领域和文本类型会有差异。它强调的是：当结论依赖专业知识实践时，仅会概括、找主旨、核对事实或记术语通常不够，还要理解该领域怎样判断证据与主张。",
      "reflection": "下一次你读一份专业材料时，除了问“它主要说什么”，还能不能再问：在这个学科里，这种证据最多有资格把结论推到哪里？"
    },
    "module": {
      "intro": "Three people can summarise the same documents almost identically yet give different answers to the question 'What does this prove?' This story separates general reading from disciplinary literacy: professional reading asks not only what a text says, but why a particular field would accept it as evidence.",
      "diagramTitle": "From understanding a document to judging a claim by disciplinary rules",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "First understand the document's surface facts and claims accurately",
        "Redistribute attention using the questions of the discipline",
        "Check the source and conditions of the evidence against disciplinary standards",
        "Limit the range of inference the evidence can support",
        "Restate the conclusion in language proportionate to the strength of the evidence"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "General comprehension is the starting point: first read the dates, numbers, events and literal meaning correctly.",
        "Different disciplines then attend to different features, such as source and chronology, measurement conditions, symbolic relationships, or the authority of a document.",
        "Disciplinary literacy is not simply noticing more details. It means understanding why a field treats certain details as central to credibility and relevance.",
        "Even when every stated fact is true, the evidence may still be insufficient for a stronger causal, responsibility-based or explanatory conclusion.",
        "The final writing should adjust the strength of its claims to match the evidence, while making clear what remains unknown and what additional material would be needed."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "The three trainee teachers produced almost identical thirty-character summaries, with all dates and figures correct → General reading ability can produce an accurate understanding of the material without yet engaging in disciplinary judgement of the evidence.",
        "Anlan checked the date, source and timing of statements in the newspaper report → Historical reading attends to sourcing, chronology and context to judge how a claim was produced.",
        "Taoyu confirmed the number was correct but still examined the sampling point, weather and limits of inference → Scientific reading asks not only whether a number is correct, but what the conditions of measurement allow the data to establish.",
        "Miko confirmed that the fine was real but distinguished a reporting violation from a finding of responsibility for pollution → Reading in legal and civic domains requires attention to a document's authority, the nature of the rule, and its evidentiary effect.",
        "Even after adding meeting minutes and a second round of testing, the article still could not name a 'culprit' → Disciplinary judgement is not ordinary fact-checking or simply adding more information; it limits claims according to the evidentiary standards of the field."
      ],
      "boundary": "Disciplinary literacy does not reject general reading strategies, nor does it assume that every discipline has only one fixed way of reading. Different tasks, subfields and text types vary. The central point is that when a conclusion depends on specialised knowledge practices, summarising, finding the main idea, checking facts or memorising terminology is usually not enough. Readers also need to understand how that field judges evidence and claims.",
      "reflection": "The next time you read a professional document, besides asking 'What is the main point?', can you also ask: in this discipline, how far is this kind of evidence actually entitled to carry the conclusion?"
    },
