      "mapping": [
        "The first three eighth-beat foot landings all carry the same woodblock note → repetition establishes an audible reference and completion point.",
        "The fourth movement now suspends beyond beat eight while the woodblock remains in its old position → the new movement conflicts with the old reference and produces premature closure.",
        "Thinning the electronic roll does not solve the problem → tension is not simply a matter of increasing or decreasing loudness and density.",
        "Removing the fourth woodblock entirely → the premature closure disappears, but the expected sound never returns, leaving the established pattern without a new completion point.",
        "The same woodblock returns at the actual wrist catch → the expectation is delayed and then completed at a new movement point; the lighting may reuse the cue, but it is not the sole evidence that the mechanism works."
      ],
      "boundary": "Tension and resolution do not mean that “louder, faster, or more suspended automatically means more tension,” and they do not require a traditional Western harmonic cadence. They depend on listeners being able to form some reference, direction, and expectation within a particular work, style, and performance. Different cultural and individual experience can produce different expectations. This story uses one contemporary dance relationship among an eight-beat crossing, a woodblock timbre, and a wrist catch; it does not make fixed eight-beat phrasing, woodblock sound, wrist contact, or Western tonal resolution into universal rules.",
      "reflection": "If this were only a technical cue moved from beat eight to the wrist catch, why would the first three woodblock landings still need to remain unchanged, and why would removing the fourth hit entirely create a different problem?"
