      "boundary": "A collective action problem does not show that participants are selfish, foolish, or unconcerned about the shared outcome, and not every scheduling problem is a collective action problem. Here, shared benefits, a real personal contribution cost, and uncertainty about others’ actions all coexist. Communication, monitoring, commitments, and rules can improve cooperation, but their effects depend on the setting. The school, community, and families in this story are fictional.",
