    "overall": "The 17:47 fire briefing is not the single authoritative answer that overturns the rumor. Before it appears, Su Yao has already weakened the headline using the 17:03 original file, the filming location, the 17:02 evacuation information, the 17:05 street footage, and the post-17:00 construction notice. The briefing adds an on-site inspection result, but even then she writes only “no structural collapse found” and “no injuries reported,” rather than converting remaining uncertainty into certainty.",
    "overallZh": "17:47消防简报不是推翻传闻的唯一答案。苏遥在它出现前，已经用17:03原文件、拍摄位置、17:02疏散信息、17:05街口视频和17:00施工通知逐步删弱标题；简报只是进一步补入现场检查结果。即使如此，她仍只写“未发现”“暂无报告”，没有把未知改写成绝对确定。",
    "module": {
      "intro": "Authentic material may support only part of a claim. Journalistic verification asks what a source actually witnessed, whether time and place match, and what independent evidence or alternative explanations remain, then matches the wording to the evidence.",
      "diagramTitle": "How a breaking claim narrows as reviewable evidence accumulates",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "Trace the claim back to the earliest publisher and obtain the original file",
        "Limit the source to what it actually encountered",
        "Check whether time, place, and content correspond",
        "Use independent material to test alternative explanations",
        "Rewrite to match the evidence and preserve what remains unknown"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "The 17:03 original file and north-side shop signs establish time and place, but directly show only evacuation and dust.",
        "The filmer did not witness a collapse or injuries, so the two stronger claims in the forwarded caption are separated from what he actually saw.",
        "The 17:02 evacuation record and 17:05 street footage add distinct points to the timeline but do not determine the cause on their own.",
        "The post-17:00 construction notice makes renovation dust a plausible alternative explanation, but not a conclusion before 17:47.",
        "The fire briefing provides an inspection result, yet the final article still uses “no structural collapse found” and “no injuries reported,” preserving the evidentiary boundary."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "The fourteen-second original video → reviewable original material with a limited information range.",
        "Successive edits in the headline field → the strength of the report changes with the evidence.",
        "17:02, 17:03, and 17:05 → timeline verification.",
        "The post-17:00 construction notice and the 17:47 fire briefing → independent material constraining an alternative explanation and the on-site conclusion.",
        "The filename “v1” → the report remains open to updating as new evidence arrives."
      ],
      "boundary": "Verification does not mean that only firsthand observation is credible, nor does it require absolute certainty. Honest eyewitnesses, mall staff, and fire officials may each know only part of an event, and multiple sources may share the same mistaken origin. What matters is a reviewable method, the limits of each source’s access, independent evidence, alternative explanations, and explicit uncertainty. The mall, reporter, and event in this story are fictional.",
      "reflection": "Why is the 17:47 fire briefing important while still being only the final piece in Su Yao’s verification chain rather than an authoritative answer that replaces all earlier checking?"
    },
