    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "On Thursday afternoon, the community archive was due to send its “1998 Workers’ Evening School” exhibition panel to print the next day. At the edge of one classroom photograph, Qin Yue noticed a woman fastening the coats of two children. In another, the same woman held a ring of small-room keys. The course register listed 28 learners and 3 teachers, while the draft panel said only, “The evening school provided childcare for learners with children.” Qin Yue placed the photographs beside the panel and tried to find out, before printing, who had been opening that room.\n\nShe first checked the original registration forms. They had fields only for learner name, course, attendance, and teacher. The expense ledger repeatedly listed milk, crayons, and folding mats, but no caregiver wages. Qin Yue had tentatively written “childcare provided by evening-school staff” on the panel, then circled the word “staff” after reading the two records. In an older file she found a destruction list: the childcare corner’s children’s sign-in sheets had been destroyed a year after the course under a privacy rule. The list showed that those sheets mainly recorded children’s names, family phone numbers, and pickup contacts; they did not have a stable field for the caregiver’s full name. Even if the sheets had survived, they might not have supplied the name she wanted.\n\nShe then checked the accession list. When the archive received the evening-school materials, it kept the course registers, teachers’ lesson plans, photographs, and financial records. Several photographs had arrived without original captions and were catalogued together as “evening-school classroom and break scenes.” No one had deliberately erased the childcare corner; the teaching register, expense records, privacy disposal, and accession choices had each served different practical purposes. Qin Yue changed the panel to “the evening school had a childcare corner,” but the name space stayed blank. The keys in the photograph became the only thing she could still follow.\n\nThat afternoon, a retired learner came in to donate her completion certificate. She recognized the photograph at once. ‘That’s Sister Xiaoqin.’ Qin Yue typed ‘Sister Xiaoqin’ into the caption, then asked for a full name. The woman remembered that she often opened the childcare corner before 19:00 and sometimes held children for learners who arrived late, but she was not sure whether Sister Xiaoqin was a learner’s relative, a temporary factory worker, or someone from the neighborhood. She could not say that she had seen her every evening. Qin Yue deleted the name she had just typed and moved it to the margin: ‘Oral name; identity unconfirmed.’ The name still gave her a new search term.\n\nNear closing time, she found two entries in a duty-and-repair notebook: “18:55 childcare-room key handed to Sister Xiaoqin” and “21:10 key returned to front desk.” The entries finally connected the name to the keys in the photographs. They were enough for Qin Yue to write, more cautiously, that “someone known as ‘Sister Xiaoqin’ took part in opening and closing the childcare corner,” but they still gave no surname, age, employment relationship, or proof that she came every day. Qin Yue deleted the last sentence on the panel that claimed too much and replaced it with: “Surviving records show that the childcare corner operated over time; the caregiver’s name and status are incompletely recorded in the archive.”\n\nThe next morning, during the final proof before printing, Qin Yue enlarged the photograph until the ring of keys was clearly visible. She did not add a full name to the woman. The footnote listed the course register, the privacy destruction list, the accession record, the duty-and-repair notebook, and the retired learner’s oral account. The final line of the panel still read: “Sister Xiaoqin — full name unconfirmed.”",
    "storyBodyZh": "周四下午，社区档案馆第二天就要把“1998工人夜校”展板送去印刷。秦悦在一张课堂照片边缘看到一位女性替两个孩子系外套，另一张里她手上挂着一串小房间钥匙。课程名册列着28名学员和3名教师，展板草稿却只写“夜校为有孩子的学员提供照看”。秦悦把照片放到展板旁边，想在送印前弄清这个开门的人是谁。\n\n她先翻原始登记表，只有学员姓名、课程、出勤和授课教师几栏；经费本连续记着牛奶、蜡笔和折叠垫，却没有照看者工资。秦悦原本在展板上试写“由夜校工作人员照看”，看完两本册子又把“工作人员”圈掉。旧文件里还有一张销毁清单：托儿角儿童签到纸一年后按隐私要求销毁。清单说明那些纸主要记孩子姓名、家庭电话和接送人，未设置固定的“照看者全名”栏，因此即使纸还在，也未必能替她补出名字。\n\n她又翻入藏清单。档案馆当年接收夜校材料时保留了课程名册、教师教案、照片和财务凭证；几张照片没有原始说明，目录便统一写成“夜校课堂与休息场景”。没有谁专门把托儿角删掉，只是教学名册、经费记录、隐私处置和入藏选择各自服务不同目的。秦悦把展板改成“夜校设有托儿角”，名字那一格仍空着，照片里的钥匙成了她下一步唯一能继续追的东西。\n\n下午，一位退休学员来送自己的结业证。她一眼认出照片：“这是小琴姐。”秦悦把“小琴姐”打进照片说明，接着追问全名。老人只记得她常在十九点前开托儿角，有时替迟到的学员抱孩子，却不确定她是学员家属、厂里临时工还是附近居民，也不能保证自己每晚都见到她。秦悦删掉刚打出的称呼，移到边栏写成：“口述称呼；身份未确认。”这个称呼却给了她新的检索词。\n\n临近下班，她在值班维修本里找到两条记录：“18:55托儿角钥匙交小琴姐”“21:10钥匙归前台”。两条记录和照片里的钥匙终于扣在一起，足以让她更谨慎地写“有人以‘小琴姐’这一称呼参与托儿角开关门”，却仍没有姓氏、年龄、工作关系，也不能证明她每天都来。秦悦删掉展板上最后一个过强的句子，改成：“现存记录显示托儿角长期运作；照看者的姓名与身份在馆藏中记录不完整。”\n\n第二天早上，送印前最后一次校样，秦悦把照片放大到能看清那串钥匙，没有给女性补上姓名。脚注列着课程名册、隐私销毁清单、入藏记录、维修本和退休学员口述。展板最下面仍留着一行：“‘小琴姐’——全名未确认。”",
    "explanation": "Archival silence can emerge gradually across record creation, administrative purpose, privacy decisions, selection for preservation, description, and later narration, leaving some experiences visible only in fragments.\n\nIndirect evidence and reading against the grain can clarify how a silence formed, but they cannot restore an absence into a complete and certain life. Unknowns and evidentiary limits should remain explicit in the final account.",
    "explanationZh": "档案沉默会在记录产生、管理用途、隐私处置、选择保存、描述和后来叙述等环节逐渐形成，使某些经验只留下侧面痕迹。\n\n旁证和逆向阅读可以帮助理解沉默怎样形成，却不能把缺席恢复成完整、确定的人生。未知与证据边界仍应明确留在最终说明里。"
