    "title": "The Padded Case Is Still on the Second Shelf",
    "summary": "As the café stacks its chairs, Zhou Yao needs to return the recorder. A padded case is in the cabinet next door, an empty tote is at hand, and he still has more than ten minutes.",
    "storyParagraphs": [
      "As the café starts stacking chairs, Zhou Yao finishes his last interview. Two microphones and a coil of cable still cover the table, and he needs to bring the studio’s main recorder back. He has more than ten minutes before the agreed return time. The padded case is on the second shelf in the temporary workroom next door. Its zipper has been stiff for months; putting the machine inside, fastening it, and packing the cable takes four extra minutes. He used to spend those minutes getting it into the case.",
      "The old checkout rule was simple: if a bump during transport caused damage that needed repair, the user paid the relevant repair cost. In October, the studio changed to an internal 80/20 arrangement: for accidental damage during normal work, the studio would cover 80% of the repair and the user 20%. The route, interview length, padded case, and stiff zipper did not change. The first time Zhou Yao signed the new form, he still packed the recorder in the case as before.",
      "Two weeks later, another Wednesday interview ends early. Zhou Yao brings the recorder back to the workroom. The padded case is still on the second shelf, and he has enough time. Looking at the empty main compartment of his canvas tote, he thinks that if the recorder is knocked during transport, he now pays only part of the repair cost. He puts the recorder directly into the tote and the two microphones into separate outer pockets. The equipment returns undamaged, and the handover still consists only of checking the item numbers, visible condition, and return time.",
      "The next week, after coiling the cable, he opens the tote, slides in the recorder, and separates the microphones into its outer pockets. His route and kit stay the same, but the padded case remains in the cabinet for several trips. At return, he sets the equipment on the counter and hangs the emptied bag by the door. The colleague checks the machines’ exterior and crosses off their numbers. Zhou Yao signs, and the log passes on. It does not ask whether padding protected them along the journey.",
      "A month later, on Wednesday, the tote hits the corner of the handover counter. The recorder still works, but its battery latch is bent and the cover will not lock securely. Replacement of the latch and a small housing part costs €60; Zhou Yao pays €12. For a separate interview the next day, with the main recorder still being repaired, he takes backup recorder B. At the location, unfamiliar shortcuts mean resetting the input gain. The interviewee has arrived, so he offers them a seat and makes another test recording.",
      "Before the next Wednesday trip, Zhou Yao puts the main recorder into the padded case and needs two tries to pull the old zipper fully closed. He folds the canvas tote and places it on the lower shelf of the cabinet. The checkout log still records only the equipment number, name, and time."
    ],
    "overall": "Moral hazard concerns how sharing the consequences of a loss may change preventive behavior that is difficult for the other party to observe. Here, under comparable route and time conditions, the same person uses an existing protective measure less often after the cost-sharing arrangement changes.",
    "conceptName": "Moral hazard",
    "explanationParagraphs": [
      "Before the new arrangement, the user bore the repair cost for transport damage. After the 80/20 split, Zhou Yao still has the same four minutes, padded case, and route, yet he starts using the tote more often. The change occurs in transport protection that the handover staff cannot observe.",
      "He still pays €12 of the €60 repair and spends extra time adapting to backup recorder B. One damaged latch cannot show that the accident probability has increased, and the new coverage is not the only possible influence on behavior."
    ],
    "module": {
      "intro": "Compare the same person before and after the protection arrangement under comparable conditions, focusing on whether hard-to-observe preventive behavior changes.",
      "diagramTitle": "Choosing the Padded Case on the Same Wednesday Route",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "Establish the pre-coverage cost responsibility and protective habit",
        "Change loss sharing while keeping the route, time, and protective equipment comparable",
        "Observe a change in the person’s transport-protection choice",
        "Record costs and time that the person still bears after damage",
        "Keep a single damage event separate from long-run risk probability"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "Under the old rule, the user paid for transport-related repairs, and Zhou Yao spent about four extra minutes using the padded case each time.",
        "After the 80/20 split, the padded case, stiff zipper, interview route, and available time remain comparable.",
        "He uses the canvas tote more often on similar Wednesday field trips, and the checkout log does not reveal that choice.",
        "He pays €12 of the €60 repair; a separate next-day interview uses backup B and requires new setup and a test recording.",
        "This damage event shows one consequence but is insufficient to infer a change in accident frequency."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "Second-shelf padded case → an available preventive measure.",
        "80/20 checkout arrangement → a change in how loss consequences are shared.",
        "Checkout log → visible pickup and return, but not transport protection.",
        "€12 out-of-pocket cost + backup-recorder setup → practical costs that remain with the user."
      ],
      "boundary": "Moral hazard does not require malice or deliberate damage, and it differs from adverse selection based on hidden type before an agreement. Behavior after protection may still be influenced by other factors; one damage event alone cannot establish that accident probability or overall risk has increased.",
      "reflection": "If the route, time, and protective equipment remain broadly unchanged, but the person reduces an unobservable precaution after loss sharing changes, what repeated behavioral evidence would you still want before concluding that the change persists?"
    },
