    "id": "0415-information-triage",
    "batchId": "batch-006",
    "categoryCode": "04",
    "fieldCode": "0415",
    "fieldTitle": "Secretarial and office work",
    "fieldTitleZh": "秘书与办公室工作",
    "mode": "mechanism-explainer",
    "title": "Four Messages Before the Doors Open at Nine",
    "summary": "The workshop opens at 9:00 with two front-desk staff. Four messages concern the side door, registrations, a receipt, and a title. Chen Shan keeps a backup sign-in sheet under the desk.",
    "storyParagraphs": [
      "At 8:20, a public workshop at a coworking space is due to open at 9:00, and the front desk has only Chen Shan and part-time colleague A-Yuan. Four messages arrive in the shared inbox at once: a wheelchair user says the automatic side door did not open during a trial visit the night before; an unfamiliar address sends a screenshot claiming that “all registration records are gone”; another participant asks for a payment receipt needed for reimbursement in three days; and the speaker asks whether two words on the title slide can be changed.",
      "Chen Shan logs all four messages. The side-door issue could affect arrival within forty minutes and can be tested immediately, so she sends A-Yuan with an access card and mechanical key while she stays at the front desk to prepare check-in. If the registration message really reflects a system-wide failure, its impact would be larger, but the screenshot’s scope is unknown. She forwards it in parallel to the remote system administrator for verification. A paper backup sign-in sheet stays under the desk rather than replacing the normal process immediately.",
      "At 8:28, A-Yuan reports that the access card works, the automatic sensor does not, and mechanical opening works normally. Chen Shan calls the building facilities duty line and receives the temporary procedure for that location: while the fault remains, staff may open the door manually from inside when someone is present, and the call bell outside the side door rings at the front desk. A-Yuan will watch the side door from 8:50 to 9:05 and then return to the main entrance; late arrivals can ring the bell and one of the two staff members can go over to open it.",
      "At 8:32, the system administrator sees 186 registrations still in the backend and successfully creates and reads one test record. There is currently no evidence that all registration data have disappeared. Chen Shan leaves the backup paper sheet under the desk and sends the screenshot sender an alternative login route. Two minutes later, that person replies that their registration is visible again. The status changes to “individual access restored / continue monitoring,” so A-Yuan no longer needs to prepare for a full manual check-in process.",
      "The speaker confirms that the title change does not affect the opening and chooses to make it on their own laptop. The receipt needed in three days is queued for completion before 14:00 that afternoon, with a reminder set. At 9:00, the participant who had flagged the accessibility need arrives at the side door, and A-Yuan opens it from inside using the procedure confirmed by facilities. At 9:05 he returns to the main entrance to help with check-in, while the side-door call bell remains available.",
      "At 13:40, Chen Shan sends the receipt and clears the 14:00 reminder. With no further registration reports by the afternoon, she records the scope of this check and ends that monitoring round. Facilities says the part will be replaced tomorrow; she leaves the side-door ticket open. As she prepares the handover, the side-door bell rings again. A-Yuan sets down the sign-in folder and goes to open it."
    ],
    "overall": "Information triage temporarily routes inputs for checking, handoff, or queuing when capacity is limited, using time sensitivity, potential impact, verifiability, and responsible roles. New evidence then updates priority, staffing, and follow-up.",
    "conceptName": "Information triage",
    "explanationParagraphs": [
      "The side-door issue directly affects arrival before 9:00 and can be checked quickly, so it receives immediate attention. The registration report has potentially wider impact and is verified in parallel. The 186 backend records, successful test write/read, and the original sender’s recovery support only the narrower conclusion that no system-wide failure is currently evident.",
      "The receipt is queued against a clear 14:00 follow-up point, while the facilities ticket remains open. The story adapts incident-response triage ideas to office work; it does not claim a universal office standard."
    ],
    "module": {
      "intro": "Triage decides what to check immediately, what to route in parallel, and what can wait, while allowing new evidence to release or reoccupy limited capacity.",
      "diagramTitle": "How Four Inputs Are Reprioritized Before 9:00",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "Log every input and preserve its original content",
        "Route each item by time sensitivity, potential impact, and verifiability",
        "Send items needing specialist confirmation to the appropriate role",
        "Use new evidence to update scope and release staff capacity",
        "Follow up queued and still-open items against explicit deadlines"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "All four messages first enter the shared tracker.",
        "A-Yuan checks the side door on site while the system administrator verifies the registration backend in parallel.",
        "Facilities confirms the temporary manual-opening procedure for this site.",
        "With 186 backend records, a successful test record, and the sender’s recovered access, the team no longer prepares for full manual registration.",
        "The receipt is sent at 13:40. That afternoon’s registration monitoring ends with its verification scope recorded; the door ticket remains open and its bell remains in use."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "Side-door message → time-sensitive and quickly verifiable high-priority input.",
        "Registration screenshot → potentially broad impact with unknown scope, requiring parallel verification.",
        "14:00 receipt reminder → lower urgency but a defined follow-up point.",
        "Side-door call bell + open facilities ticket → temporary workaround and unresolved repair coexist."
      ],
      "boundary": "Triage is temporary classification and routing, not a final truth judgment, and it does not require waiting for serious harm before acting. A currently healthy backend and one user’s restored access do not prove that all future access will be problem-free. NIST and FIRST sources come from cybersecurity incident response; this story uses them only as an office-work analogy.",
      "reflection": "When one issue could have very broad impact but its scope is unknown, while another smaller issue will block someone within forty minutes, how can parallel verification avoid both shutting down the whole process unnecessarily and neglecting the immediate problem?"
    },
