    "overall": "Database normalization is illustrated here through fictional rental data: relations are decomposed according to functional dependencies and the meaning of facts, then reconnected through keys to reduce update, insertion, and deletion anomalies caused by maintaining the same fact in multiple places. Current customer data and historical order snapshots are different facts, and decomposition still requires lossless relationships and necessary constraints to be preserved.",
    "module": {
      "intro": "Use the original receipt to separate “where the customer is now” from “where this order was delivered then,” then decompose the data according to functional dependencies and reconnect it.",
      "diagramTitle": "Why the Current Address Changes Once While the Historical Order Keeps Its Old Street",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "Identify that the old single table repeats current customer information across multiple rentals",
        "Use the original R551 signed receipt to confirm that the historical delivery address is independent of the current address",
        "Separate current Customer facts from Rental transaction snapshots and reconnect them through CustomerID",
        "Update C208 once while R551 retains Pine Street and R604 stores River Road",
        "Check the migration against original documents, join queries, and foreign-key constraints"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "The problem is not that all repetition is wrong; it is that one current fact is independently maintained in multiple places.",
        "A historical snapshot must come from a record made at the time, not be reconstructed from the current address.",
        "The old table has the transitive dependency RentalID→CustomerID→current customer attributes; the historical delivery snapshot still depends on its own RentalID.",
        "A change in current customer information should not rewrite completed transaction snapshots.",
        "Checking four records validates this teaching migration instance only; it is not a general proof of lossless decomposition."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "C208 → the identifying key for current customer attributes.",
        "R551 signed receipt → independent evidence for the Pine Street historical snapshot.",
        "R604 → today’s order storing the River Road snapshot.",
        "Foreign key → a relational constraint preventing a rental from referencing a nonexistent customer."
      ],
      "boundary": "Normalization does not require eliminating every repetition, nor does more tables automatically mean better design. Historical snapshots, audit records, and controlled redundancy can have independent business value; decomposition must preserve lossless joins and necessary constraints. Codd’s 1970 paper established the relational model and discussed the normal form of that period; it should not be described as already containing the complete later theory of 2NF, 3NF, and all subsequent normal forms. In this example, CustomerID is unique in Customer and is a non-null foreign key in Rental, so each rental joins to one existing customer record. Checking four records still does not replace lossless-join analysis for a general design.",
      "reflection": "Why should Zhou Yao’s current address in Customer change to River Road, while Pine Street in R551 must remain unchanged?"
    },
