    "collection": "field-core-fables",
    "storyBody": "On the morning merchant Lu Ming was due to leave for his daughter's wedding, a corner of an old filling caught on thread. He went to Dr. Meng, who had treated that tooth three times in twelve years. Each earlier visit had ended the same way: a dark margin looked suspicious, the old material came out, and a larger clean-edged filling went in. Lu Ming had always said that new material must be safer than old. The work was efficient and easy to explain. Dr. Meng's appointment book showed a crown-sized gap that afternoon. If she prepared the tooth now, he could return from the wedding to something new and strong.\n\nLu Ming wanted certainty. He had negotiated three months for the wedding banquet and did not want to count every bite in front of his guests. Dr. Meng pointed to a plaster model made before the first filling. On it, the repaired area was a small circle. The current radiograph and photograph showed three wider circles, one inside another, like rings cut into a tree. Only one edge had chipped this time; much of the latest restoration and the surrounding tooth appeared serviceable. Still, replacing everything would be faster than explaining why leaving old material could sometimes be deliberate.\n\nDr. Meng opened the crown kit. Her assistant, Tao, set the smallest repair bur beside it without speaking. Twelve years earlier, Tao had also passed her the first bur. Dr. Meng remembered how each complete replacement had removed not only filling but another rim of sound tooth needed for access and shape. The previous success had hidden its cost because the tooth looked whole when Lu Ming left. Now one wall was thin enough to glow under the lamp.\n\nShe almost followed the familiar route. A crown could protect a substantially weakened tooth in the right case, and preserving tissue did not mean refusing necessary restoration. But her assessment had not found a fracture or loss that justified removing every remaining wall that morning. The active problem was a local defect. The larger risks—new decay, difficult cleaning, repeated loading, and future fracture—would also need attention, whichever material she placed.\n\nLu Ming objected when Dr. Meng proposed a localized repair, risk management, and review. 'You replaced it last time. Was that wrong?' he asked. The waiting room went quiet. Dr. Meng looked at the crown kit, closed its lid, and placed the first model beside the newest image. She said the old choices had bought years of service but also enlarged the next decision. She could not put the removed rings back. She could only avoid taking another without a reason.\n\nThey chose the smaller repair after discussing its uncertainty and alternatives. Dr. Meng lost the crown fee, kept an extra review slot unpaid, and stayed late because the explanation took longer than the procedure. Lu Ming missed the first train and paid to change his ticket. At the wedding he avoided the hardest sugared nuts until the repaired edge had been reviewed; the inconvenience was real, and nobody called it a perfect solution.\n\nA year later the repair remained serviceable, while Dr. Meng continued to check the lesion activity, margins, cleaning, and structure rather than promising permanence. The plaster model stayed on her shelf. She painted the first three circles in gray and left the fourth circle only in pencil. Each time she reached for a large bur, the unfinished line was the first thing under her fingers.",
    "storyBodyZh": "商人陆明要去参加女儿婚礼的早晨，一根牙线勾住了旧填料边角。他赶到孟医生诊所，这颗牙十二年里已处理过三次。每次都很相似：边缘出现黑线，旧填料被整块取出，再换成更大、更整齐的新填料。陆明总说新东西比旧东西可靠，这样处理也快而好解释。孟医生下午正好空着一段足够做牙冠的时间。若现在预备，陆明婚礼回来就能得到一颗看起来又新又结实的牙。\n\n陆明想要确定。他为婚宴谈了三个月生意，不愿当着宾客的面每咬一口都提心吊胆。孟医生拿出第一次修复前留下的石膏牙模，上面的填补区只是一个小圆。如今的照片和影像里，三个更大的圆圈套在一起，像树干年轮。这次只有一处边角崩落，大部分旧材料和周围牙体仍可使用。可整块更换比解释“为什么有时故意留下旧材料”省时得多。\n\n孟医生打开牙冠器械盒。助手小陶没有说话，只把最细的修补钻针放在旁边。十二年前，第一根钻针也是小陶递来的。孟医生想起，每次完整更换不仅拿走填料，还会为了进入、成形，再削去一圈健康牙体。过去的成功把代价藏了起来，因为陆明每次离开时，牙齿看上去都完整。如今，一侧薄壁在灯下已经透出亮光。\n\n她差点沿着熟路继续。牙冠在牙体确实大幅削弱时可以有必要，保存组织也不等于拒绝该做的修复。可这次个体检查没有发现足以支持当天磨除所有牙壁的断裂或缺损，眼前只是局部崩边。无论选择什么材料，新的龋坏、难以清洁、反复受力和未来断裂等风险，也不能靠一次“大工程”自动消失。\n\n听到局部修补、风险管理和复查的方案，陆明皱起眉：“你上次明明整块换了。那次做错了吗？”候诊室忽然安静。孟医生看了一眼牙冠器械盒，把盒盖合上，再将最初牙模与最新照片并排。她说过去的选择换来了若干年使用，也把下一次选择越做越大。已经拿走的圆圈无法放回去，她现在能做的，是不再无故多拿一圈。\n\n在讨论不确定性与替代方案后，两人选择了较小的修补。孟医生放弃牙冠费用，额外留下一个不收费的复查时段，还因解释比操作更久而延迟下班。陆明错过第一班车，花钱改签；婚宴上，他在复查前没有碰最硬的糖果仁。麻烦和代价都真实存在，没有人把这叫作完美结局。\n\n一年后，修补仍可使用，孟医生继续观察病变活动、边缘、清洁与剩余结构，没有许诺永久。那只石膏牙模一直放在诊室架上。她把前三个圆圈涂成灰色，第四圈只留了一道铅笔线。以后每次伸手取大钻针，她的指尖都会先碰到那条没刻下去的线。",
    "explanation": "Minimal intervention dentistry aims to detect disease early, control causes and risk, and preserve sound tooth structure across a lifetime. Depending on lesion activity, cleanability, structural integrity, patient factors, and uncertainty, care may include prevention, monitoring, non-operative or micro-invasive measures, selective tissue removal, or repair of a serviceable restoration. It does not mean doing nothing, always choosing the smallest procedure, or avoiding a necessary restoration. Replacing a restoration can begin a cycle of progressively larger treatment, so intervention should be proportionate to the diagnosed problem and revisited over time.",
